    {
     "kind": "grammar",
     "src": "p.25",
     "topic": "Introduction to roots and patterns",
     "note": "Arabic verbs (and words in general) are a combination of a root and a pattern. The root usually consists of 3 consonants, and the pattern is a template for adding consonants and/or vowels (as prefixes, affixes and/or suffixes) to the root letters. Each root is used in more than one pattern, and each pattern is used for more than one root. The combination of root and pattern is what gives a word its specific semantic meaning. Example: the root و.ل.د combined with the pattern x-x́-x (fatha on 1st and 2nd radicals) forms وَلَد 'boy'; the same root in the pattern x-x-'alif-x́ gives a word with a different meaning: وَالِد 'parent'.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ar": "وَلَد",
       "en": "boy"
      },
      {
       "ar": "وَالِد",
       "en": "parent"
      }
     ],
     "table": null
    },
    {
     "kind": "grammar",
     "src": "p.25",
     "topic": "The conjugation of verbs in the perfect form (singular only)",
     "note": "The simplest form of a verb is the 3rd person masculine singular of the perfect. For this person in the simple trilateral verb (a verb with a root of three radicals), the first and the third radicals are vocalized with a fatha. The second radical may be vocalized with a fatha (most common), kasra or (rarely) damma. The perfect form is conjugated by adding endings to the radicals of the verb (table: person / example / ending).",
     "examples": [],
