    {
     "kind": "grammar",
     "src": "p.26",
     "topic": "Word order in verbal sentences",
     "note": "1. If the subject of the sentence is an independent pronoun (I, he, she, etc.) and the predicate is a verb, the verb will follow its subject. 2. If the subject of the sentence is a noun and the predicate is a verb, the verb will usually precede its subject and agree with it in gender. (Footnote: It is allowed to place the verb after its subject in order to emphasize the subject or as a stylistic device. This word order is common in headlines of newspapers: أَلْوَزِير زَارَ لُبْنَان - the minister visited Lebanon.) 3. In a sentence that includes more than one verb, the first verb will precede its subject, and the other verbs will follow it.",
     "examples": [
      {
       "ar": "أَنَا نَزَلْتُ",
       "en": "I descended"
      },
